# Niagara Asset Bootstrap API Map

Status: research baseline plus first-pass UE 5.6.1 Niagara creation support in the local UnrealMCP fork.

Goal: learn and document how Unreal Engine itself creates valid Niagara System and Emitter assets before adding MCP creation commands. The MCP plugin must remain an authoring tool only; generated Niagara assets must not serialize any `/Script/UnrealMCP`, `/UnrealMCP`, `UnrealMCP`, `MCPUnreal`, or `mcp_unreal` dependency.

## Scope

This note covers the engine-side API surface needed for:

- creating an empty Niagara System asset,
- creating an empty Niagara Emitter asset,
- creating a basic visible sprite Niagara System,
- creating conservative rain/snow sprite presets with stock BoxLocation, force/noise/color/size modules, and InitializeParticle size/lifetime tuning,
- inspecting factory defaults without saving source assets,
- previewing generated Niagara systems through transient actors without saving or reloading maps,
- validating that generated content remains normal `/Script/Niagara` content.

The first implementation slice should target `/Game/_MCP_Temp/NiagaraGenerated/` only.

## Engine API Map

### Saved Asset Creation Path

Headers:

- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Source\Developer\AssetTools\Public\IAssetTools.h`
- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Source\Developer\AssetTools\Private\AssetTools.cpp`

Key API:

- `IAssetTools::CreateAsset(const FString& AssetName, const FString& PackagePath, UClass* AssetClass, UFactory* Factory, FName CallingContext=NAME_None)`
- `UAssetToolsImpl::CanCreateAsset(...)`

Observed UE 5.6.1 flow:

- `CreateAsset` guards the factory from GC, verifies the asset class/factory pair, sanitizes the long package name, then calls `CanCreateAsset`.
- `CanCreateAsset` validates object/package names, rejects map-name collisions, checks path allow rules, fully loads the package, and handles existing-object collisions.
- In unattended mode, an existing object collision returns `false` instead of opening an overwrite prompt.
- After validation, `CreateAsset` creates the package, calls `Factory->FactoryCreateNew(...)` with `RF_Public | RF_Standalone | RF_Transactional`, registers `FAssetRegistryModule::AssetCreated(NewObj)`, and marks the package dirty.
- Niagara's own baker helper uses this pattern by creating a transient factory object and calling `AssetTools.CreateAsset(...)`.

Engine source evidence:

- `IAssetTools.h:342`: `CreateAsset` declaration.
- `AssetTools.cpp:1717-1775`: `CreateAsset` implementation.
- `AssetTools.cpp:4595-4725`: `CanCreateAsset` validation and existing-object behavior.
- `NiagaraBakerOutput.cpp:98-127`: Niagara helper that creates a transient factory and delegates to `AssetTools.CreateAsset`.

Implication for MCP:

- Use `AssetTools.CreateAsset` for first-pass saved Niagara System and Emitter creation instead of manually creating packages and duplicating partial object graphs.
- Instantiate `UNiagaraSystemFactoryNew` or `UNiagaraEmitterFactoryNew` under `GetTransientPackage()`, set automation fields, then pass the factory to `CreateAsset`.
- Reject existing target assets by default. Do not implement overwrite in the first pass because the editor path would normally prompt, while MCP runs unattended.
- `CreateAsset` already registers the asset and marks the package dirty; the MCP command still needs explicit compile/status handling, save, and post-save dependency audit.

### System Factory

Header:

- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Plugins\FX\Niagara\Source\NiagaraEditor\Public\NiagaraSystemFactoryNew.h`

Key API:

- `UNiagaraSystemFactoryNew::FactoryCreateNew(...)`
- `UNiagaraSystemFactoryNew::InitializeSystem(UNiagaraSystem* System, bool bCreateDefaultNodes)`
- Public fields:
  - `UNiagaraSystem* SystemToCopy`
  - `TArray<FVersionedNiagaraEmitter> EmittersToAddToNewSystem`

Observed UE 5.6.1 flow:

- If `SystemToCopy` is set, the factory waits for source compilation and duplicates the source system.
- If `EmittersToAddToNewSystem` is non-empty, the factory creates a new `UNiagaraSystem`, calls `InitializeSystem(NewSystem, true)`, then adds each emitter through `FNiagaraEditorUtilities::AddEmitterToSystem`.
- If neither is set, the factory creates a new `UNiagaraSystem` and calls `InitializeSystem(NewSystem, true)`.
- `InitializeSystem` creates a shared `UNiagaraScriptSource` and `UNiagaraGraph`, assigns it to the system spawn/update scripts, resets system spawn/update output nodes, and optionally adds the editor-configured required system update module.
- The factory assigns a default effect type when settings provide one, then calls `NewSystem->RequestCompile(false)`.

Engine source evidence:

- `NiagaraSystemFactoryNew.cpp:80-147`: factory create/duplicate/add-emitter/new-system decision.
- `NiagaraSystemFactoryNew.cpp:149-182`: system script source, graph, output nodes, and default system update module initialization.

Implication for MCP:

- Use the engine factory path or the same static initializer rather than constructing a package and partially initializing scripts by hand.
- For an "empty system" command, `InitializeSystem(System, true)` matches editor behavior better than `false`.
- For a "basic sprite system" command, prefer creating an emitter through `UNiagaraEmitterFactoryNew` and then adding it to the system.

### Emitter Factory

Header:

- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Plugins\FX\Niagara\Source\NiagaraEditor\Public\NiagaraEmitterFactoryNew.h`

Key API:

- `UNiagaraEmitterFactoryNew::FactoryCreateNew(...)`
- `UNiagaraEmitterFactoryNew::InitializeEmitter(UNiagaraEmitter* NewEmitter, bool bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderers)`
- Public fields:
  - `UNiagaraEmitter* EmitterToCopy`
  - `bool bUseInheritance`
  - `bool bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderersToEmptyEmitter`

Observed UE 5.6.1 flow:

- If `EmitterToCopy` is set and inheritance is enabled, the factory uses `UNiagaraEmitter::CreateWithParentAndOwner`.
- If `EmitterToCopy` is set and inheritance is disabled, the factory duplicates the source emitter, assigns a unique emitter name, and disables versioning.
- If no source emitter is set, the factory creates a new `UNiagaraEmitter` and calls `InitializeEmitter(NewEmitter, bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderersToEmptyEmitter)`.
- The constructor default for `bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderersToEmptyEmitter` is `true`.
- The interactive `ConfigureProperties` path sets `bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderersToEmptyEmitter=false` only when no source emitter and no configured default empty emitter are selected.
- `InitializeEmitter` creates emitter graph source and graph, assigns the source to spawn/update/emitter spawn/emitter update/GPU scripts, creates output nodes for all standard emitter script usages, and sets spawn script usage to `ParticleSpawnScriptInterpolated`.
- With default modules enabled, it adds:
  - one sprite renderer,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Emitter/EmitterState.EmitterState`,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Emitter/SpawnRate.SpawnRate` with `SpawnRate=10`,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Spawn/Location/SystemLocation.SystemLocation`,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Spawn/Velocity/AddVelocity.AddVelocity` with `Velocity=(0,0,100)`,
  - a parameter module for sprite size, sprite rotation, and lifetime,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Update/Lifetime/UpdateAge.UpdateAge`,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Update/Color/Color.Color`,
  - `/Niagara/Modules/Solvers/SolveForcesAndVelocity.SolveForcesAndVelocity`.

Engine source evidence:

- `NiagaraEmitterFactoryNew.cpp:125-167`: factory copy/inheritance/empty-emitter decision.
- `NiagaraEmitterFactoryNew.cpp:169-245`: emitter graph/script source setup and default sprite emitter modules.

Implication for MCP:

- For a truly empty emitter, set `bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderersToEmptyEmitter=false` explicitly before calling `AssetTools.CreateAsset`.
- `bAddDefaultModulesAndRenderersToEmptyEmitter=true` is the quickest safe path to a visible basic sprite emitter without hand-authoring the graph.
- The current module-input and renderer MCP tools can tune the generated sprite emitter after factory creation.

### Adding Emitters To Systems

Headers:

- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Plugins\FX\Niagara\Source\Niagara\Classes\NiagaraSystem.h`
- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Plugins\FX\Niagara\Source\Niagara\Classes\NiagaraEmitter.h`
- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Plugins\FX\Niagara\Source\NiagaraEditor\Public\NiagaraEditorUtilities.h`

Key API:

- `FNiagaraEditorUtilities::AddEmitterToSystem(UNiagaraSystem& InSystem, UNiagaraEmitter& InEmitterToAdd, FGuid EmitterVersion, bool bCreateCopy=true)`
- `UNiagaraSystem::AddEmitterHandle(UNiagaraEmitter& SourceEmitter, FName EmitterName, FGuid EmitterVersion)`
- `UNiagaraSystem::AddEmitterHandleDirect(FNiagaraEmitterHandle& EmitterHandleToAdd)`
- `UNiagaraSystem::DuplicateEmitterHandle(...)`
- `UNiagaraEmitter::CreateWithParentAndOwner(...)`
- `UNiagaraEmitter::CreateAsDuplicate(...)`

Observed UE 5.6.1 flow:

- `FNiagaraEditorUtilities::AddEmitterToSystem` kills live system instances before mutating the emitter handle list, gathers existing emitter names, creates a unique emitter handle name, calls `InSystem.Modify()`, then uses `UNiagaraSystem::AddEmitterHandle` for normal system assets.
- After adding the handle, `AddEmitterToSystem` rebuilds emitter nodes and synchronizes the system overview graph.
- `AddEmitterHandle` creates an owned emitter copy through `UNiagaraEmitter::CreateWithParentAndOwner`.
- It adds an `FNiagaraEmitterHandle`, refreshes system parameters from the emitter, and invalidates cached data.
- `AddEmitterHandleDirect` is explicitly documented for temporary systems only and should not be used for live assets.
- `RefreshSystemParametersFromEmitter` copies emitter spawn/update RapidIteration parameters into the system spawn/update RapidIteration stores while the emitter handle is enabled.
- `UNiagaraEmitter::CreateWithParentAndOwner` duplicates the source emitter into the system, disables versioning for the chosen version, sets the unique emitter name, records parent metadata, duplicates the last-merge parent, and marks the graph source not synchronized.
- `UNiagaraEmitter::bIsInheritable` defaults to `true`.
- `UNiagaraSystem::AddEmitterHandle` removes parent metadata only when the source emitter is not inheritable.

Engine source evidence:

- `NiagaraEditorUtilities.cpp:2102-2157`: `AddEmitterToSystem`.
- `NiagaraSystem.cpp:2819-2833`: `AddEmitterHandle`.
- `NiagaraSystem.cpp:2835-2840`: `AddEmitterHandleDirect`.
- `NiagaraSystem.cpp:1508-1530`: `RefreshSystemParametersFromEmitter`.
- `NiagaraEmitter.cpp:1108-1122`: `CreateWithParentAndOwner`.
- `NiagaraEmitter.cpp:1126-1151`: `CreateAsDuplicate`.
- `NiagaraEmitter.h:741`: default `bIsInheritable=true`.
- `NiagaraEmitter.cpp:3681-3685`: `FVersionedNiagaraEmitterData::RemoveParent`.

Implication for MCP:

- For saved generated systems, prefer `FNiagaraEditorUtilities::AddEmitterToSystem` over direct handle mutation because it handles live instance teardown, name uniqueness, emitter node rebuild, and overview graph synchronization.
- Avoid `AddEmitterHandleDirect` for saved content.
- Do not hand-copy RapidIteration parameters when adding an emitter through the engine API; `AddEmitterHandle` already refreshes system parameter stores.
- A visible basic sprite system should use a transient source emitter initialized through `UNiagaraEmitterFactoryNew::InitializeEmitter(..., true)`, but set that source emitter to `bIsInheritable=false` before adding it to the system. This forces `AddEmitterHandle` to remove parent metadata from the owned emitter copy and avoids saving a dependency on a transient or temp source emitter.
- A saved temp emitter asset is useful for debugging only. It creates an extra dependency that must be audited away before promotion outside `/Game/_MCP_Temp`.

### Compile And Save Flow

Observed UE 5.6.1 flow:

- `UNiagaraSystem::RequestCompile(bool bForce, ...)` runs on the game thread, returns `false` on dedicated server/cooked editor packages/filtered editor-only packages/reentrant cases, and launches active compilation work when needed.
- If no compilation launches and one active compilation exists, `RequestCompile` polls once; otherwise callers still need to inspect or wait for completion separately.
- `UNiagaraSystem::PollForCompilationComplete(bool bFlushRequestCompile)` can flush a pending request before checking completion.
- `UNiagaraSystem::WaitForCompilationComplete(...)` flushes pending requests through `RequestCompile(false)` before blocking on active compile jobs.
- `UEditorAssetSubsystem::SaveLoadedAsset` requires the object to be a registered asset, then delegates to `UEditorLoadingAndSavingUtils::SavePackages`, which runs unattended and can perform source-control checkout before saving.
- The existing local MCP compile-status tool already wraps compile request and wait behavior with generated/temp guards.
- The existing local direct save helper rejects invalid/transient packages, validates long package names, converts package names to filenames, saves with `RF_Public | RF_Standalone`, and clears dirty state on success.

Engine source evidence:

- `NiagaraSystem.cpp:3593-3659`: `RequestCompile`.
- `NiagaraSystem.cpp:3087-3095`: `PollForCompilationComplete`.
- `NiagaraSystem.cpp:2989-3064`: `WaitForCompilationComplete`.
- `EditorAssetSubsystem.cpp:1288-1307`: `SaveLoadedAsset`.
- `FileHelpers.cpp:5559-5579` and `FileHelpers.cpp:5644-5648`: unattended `SavePackages` path.
- `MCPGameProject/Plugins/UnrealMCP/Source/UnrealMCP/Private/Commands/UnrealMCPProjectCommands.cpp:394-436`: local `SaveLoadedAssetForMCP` package-save pattern.

Implication for MCP:

- Creation commands should call `RequestCompile(false)` only after all initial graph/emitter mutations are complete.
- The command response should report `compile_requested` and either return immediately or reuse the existing compile-status polling pattern when `wait_for_completion=true`.
- Saving should use normal package save semantics (`RF_Public | RF_Standalone`, valid long package name, not transient), never MCP-owned classes or objects.
- For the first pass, prefer a local direct save helper in `UnrealMCPNiagaraCommands.cpp` that mirrors `SaveLoadedAssetForMCP`, so the command behavior is deterministic and does not silently depend on source-control checkout.
- If future workflow needs checkout-aware saves, switch deliberately to `UEditorAssetSubsystem::SaveLoadedAsset` after confirming registered-asset status from the `AssetTools.CreateAsset` path.

### Dependency Audit Path

Header:

- `C:\Program Files\Epic Games\UE_5.6\Engine\Source\Runtime\AssetRegistry\Public\AssetRegistry\IAssetRegistry.h`

Key API:

- `IAssetRegistry::GetDependencies(FName PackageName, TArray<FName>& OutDependencies, EDependencyCategory Category, const FDependencyQuery& Flags)`
- `FAssetRegistryDependencyOptions`

Observed UE 5.6.1 flow:

- AssetRegistry package dependency queries return dependencies reported in the on-disk package.
- `FAssetRegistryDependencyOptions` includes soft package references, hard package references, searchable names, and management references.

Engine source evidence:

- `IAssetRegistry.h:132-155`: dependency option fields.
- `IAssetRegistry.h:518-539`: dependency query overloads.

Implication for MCP:

- Run dependency audit after saving, not only on the in-memory package, because AssetRegistry dependency results are on-disk package reports.
- Check both hard and soft package dependencies for forbidden MCP markers.
- Also scan generated object/package path strings for `/Script/UnrealMCP`, `/UnrealMCP`, `UnrealMCP`, `MCPUnreal`, and `mcp_unreal`, because not every problematic marker is necessarily a package dependency.
- For promotion outside temp, reject dependencies back into `/Game/_MCP_Temp` unless the user explicitly approves that exact dependency.

